by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T07:13:59Z

One more pass over the batch with a narrower question: which commits change behavior someone could
depend on, beyond removing a defect. Six do. I checked what consumes each one inside the component and
none of them relies on the old behavior, but they are the commits to read twice before merging, so here
they are with measurements.

**#65406, `TickScheduler`: the delivered rate changes.** Rescheduling from the poll time meant every
late poll stretched the period for good. Anchoring to the schedule changes how often callbacks fire:
a 100ms interval polled every 60ms delivers 83 calls in 10s on 8.1 and 99 on this branch. Code that
counts invocations, or that was tuned against the slower effective rate, sees the difference.
`ScheduledTickTrait` drives widget animations from it and only redraws, so nothing in the component
cares, but the class is public.

**#65413, `FixedStepAccumulator`: the meaning of `maxStepsPerUpdate` changes.** It was a rate limiter
over a preserved backlog; it is now a cap that discards what it rejects. After a 2s stall at 10 steps/s
with a cap of 5, both versions return 5 steps at the stall, but the following second yields 24 steps on
8.1 (the backlog paying out) and 9 on this branch. Total steps over time is no longer conserved. For
animations that is the point; a consumer using it to pace real work would now lose work. `LoaderWidget`
and `ProgressBarWidget` animations are the only consumers here.

**#65410, `Compositor`: an exception becomes an empty result.** A base layer with no lines made
`CellBuffer` throw; `composite()` now returns `[]`, and whatever the upper layers contained is silently
gone. Defensible because the first layer is documented as defining the canvas, and nothing in the
component calls `composite()` today, but it is an error signal removed from a public method.

**#65411, `ScreenBufferHtmlRenderer`: an exception becomes a skip.** An out-of-range 38/48/58 color
parameter made the whole conversion throw; it now drops that color and keeps the rest of the sequence. Right for replayed terminal
output, which is untrusted by nature, but anyone using the exception to validate input loses it.

**#65399, `EditorDocument`: undo granularity.** A multi-line paste was one undo step per line and is now
a single step. This matches what a user means by "undo the paste", and it is what the commit is for, but
it is a user-visible change in editing behavior, not a repaired computation.

**#65414, `SettingsListWidget`: cycling forward from a value the list does not offer now lands on the
first value instead of the second.** The old code read the missed lookup as index 0 and stepped from
there. The new behavior matches what activating the same item does, which is the argument for it.

The remaining thirteen commits repair computations whose results were plainly wrong (width arithmetic,
byte and column confusion, missing guards) and change nothing anyone could sensibly depend on.

All of this is on an `@experimental` component, so none of it is a BC promise. My conclusion is that all
six are correct calls; this comment exists so the decision is made knowingly rather than by merge.

